          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A left/right
            %em exact functor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
            = succeed "/finite" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          %p
            The term originates in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OriginOfTerminology"}
            below, where a central role is played by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
            (originally of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            preserve or destroy exactness of sequences to some extent gave vital information on those functors.
          %p
            In this context, one says that an
            %strong exact functor
            is one that preserves exact sequences. However, many functors are only “exact on one side or the other”. For instance, for all modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequences"} short exact sequences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A \to B \to C \to 0
            of modules (over some ring
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to Mod_R(M, A) \to Mod_R(M,B) \to Mod_R(M,C)
          %p
            is exact – but note that there is no 0 on the right hand. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(-) = Mod_R(M,-)
            converts an exact sequence into a
            %em left
            exact sequence; such a functor is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong left exact functor
            = succeed "." do
              %strong right exact functors
          %p
            It is easy to see that an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive functor
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+categories"} additive categories
            is left exact in this sense if and only if it preserves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
          %p
            Since merely preserving left exact sequences does not require a functor to be additive, in a non-additive context one defines a
            %strong left exact functor
            to be one which preserves finite limits, and dually. Below we give the
            %a{:href => "#Definition"} general definition
            and then discuss the relation to the concept in homological algebra in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#OnAbelianCategories"} Properties - On abelian categories
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+categories"} finitely complete categories
            is called
            %strong left exact
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"} flat
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            %strong right exact
            if it preserves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+colimits"} finite colimits
            %strong exact
            if it is both left and right exact.
          %p
            Specifically,
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            are exact if they preserve
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#characterisations Characterisations
          %h4#general General
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
                  between finitely cocomplete categories is right exact if and only if for all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F/d
                  is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+category"} filtered
              %li
                %p
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
                  between finitely complete categories is left exact if and only if for all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi F
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d/F)^{op}
                  is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+category"} filtered
          %p
            In other language, this says that a functor between finitely complete categories is left exact if and only if it is (representably)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"} flat
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A functor between categories with finite limits preserves
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite limit
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  it preserves
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+objects"} terminal objects
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                  = succeed "s;" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
              %li
                %p
                  it preserves
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p Since these conditions frequently come up individually, it may be worthwhile listing them separately:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
                %strong preserves terminal objects
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(t_C)
                is terminal in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_C
                is terminal in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
                %strong preserves binary products
                if the pair of maps
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ×
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c) \stackrel{F(\pi_1)}{\leftarrow} F(c \times d) \stackrel{F(\pi_2)}{\to} F(d)
              %p
                exhibits
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ×
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c \times d)
                as a product of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c)
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(d)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo ×
                      %mi d
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: c \times d \to c
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo ×
                      %mi d
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2: c \times d \to d
                are the product projections in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
                %strong preserves equalizers
                if the map
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(i): F(e) \to F(c)
              %p
                is the equalizer of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f), F(g): F(c) \stackrel{\to}{\to} F(d)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi e
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: e \to c
                is the equalizer of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g: c \stackrel{\to}{\to} d
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_remark
            %h6#terminology Terminology
            %p
              Some author use the term “left exact” when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              does not have all
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"} flat functor
            %p
              ‘Left exact’ is sometimes abbreviated
              = succeed "." do
                %strong lex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lex"} Lex
              is the 2-category of categories with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              and lex functors. See also
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functor"} continuous functor
              = succeed "." do
                %strong rex
            %p
              Left exact functors correspond to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-representable+functors"} pro-representable functors
          %h4#between_categories_of_modules Between categories of modules
          %p
            Right exact functors between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            are characterized by the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
          %h3#OnAbelianCategories On abelian categories / in homological algebra
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequences"} short exact sequences
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
              is left exact if and only if it preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums"} direct sums
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernels"} kernels
            %p
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
              is right exact if and only if it preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums"} direct sums
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernels"} cokernels
          #ExactFunctorPreservesExactSequences.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              In particular for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A \to B \to C \to 0
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
              in the abelian category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is left exact then
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        0 \to F(A) \to F(B) \to F(C)
                %p
                  is an exact sequence in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi D
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is right exact then
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        F(A) \to F(B) \to F(C) \to 0
                %p
                  is an exact sequence in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi D
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is exact then
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        0 \to F(A) \to F(B) \to F(C) \to 0
                %p
                  is an exact sequence in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi D
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %p
              Also: if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is exact then it preserves
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p We discuss the first case. The second is formally dual. The third combines the two cases.
            %p
              For the first case notice that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A \stackrel{i}{\to} B \stackrel{p}{\to} C \to 0
              being an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
              is equivalent to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              being a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              being an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A
              being the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              being the kernel of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to 0
              being the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is assumed to preserve this kernel-property, but not the cokernel property, it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(0) \to F(A)
              is the kernel of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A) \stackrel{F(i)}{\to} F(B)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(i)
              is the kernel of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(p)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \to F(A) \to F(B) \to F(C)
            %p is an exact sequence, as claimed.
          #OriginOfTerminology.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The properties of corollary
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExactFunctorPreservesExactSequences"}
              explain the “left”/“right”-terminology: a
              %em left
              exact functor preserves exactness of sequences to the left of a morphism (only), while a
              %em right
              exact functor preserves exactness to the right.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LexCat"} LexCat
            %li
              %p
